  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m spooling. Persisting logs across sessions means storing potentially-sensitive records on the user's
  disk, which reopens the privacy question we just closed, and it buys reliability you don't need for diagnostics. If a
  batch is lost, you lose a few lines, not the ability to debug.

  [1mNode sink.[0m One JSON object per line to stdout, [38;5;153merror[39m to stderr. Synchronous, unbatched, no transport — whatever
  supervises the process collects it. This is the cheap side and should stay cheap.

  [1mGlobal handlers[0m ([38;5;153mhandlers.js[39m, installed explicitly by [38;5;153mbrowser.js[39m/[38;5;153mnode.js[39m, never as an import side effect — implicit
  global installation makes tests order-dependent and surprises anyone importing the core):

  ┌─────────┬─────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Runtime │              Hooks              │                              Behavior                               │
  ├─────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Browser │ [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m,                 │ Log at [38;5;153merror[39m with the scrubbed stack, then re-dispatch to any       │
  │         │ [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m              │ previously registered handler                                       │
  ├─────────┼─────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Node    │ [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m,              │ Log at [38;5;153merror[39m, flush synchronously, then [1mre-throw / preserve default[0m │
  │         │ [38;5;153munhandledRejection[39m              │ [1m exit behavior[0m                                                      │
  └─────────┴─────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  That last point matters: a handler that swallows [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m turns a crash into a zombie process. We log and
  then let the process die as it would have.

  [1mConfig surface[0m, one object at construction, no globals:

  [33mcreateLogger[39m({ level, release, endpoint, apiKey, allowlist, sink })

  The browser's [38;5;153mapiKey[39m is a write-only vendor ingest key, and it ships in client-side JavaScript where anyone can read
  it — that's inherent to browser telemetry, not a flaw we can design away. It must be an ingest-scoped key with no read
  access, and it needs to be rotatable. I'd have the spec state that as a requirement on whichever vendor you pick,
  since not all of them offer write-only keys.

  Does Section 3 look right?

